# instance/.gitignore — re-includes the overlay's KNOWN entries under the root rule `/instance/*`, so an
# INSTANCE repository (and the control node's canonical) tracks them. Scaffolded by `kontroll-init --fresh`
# from instance.example/.gitignore; add a line when you add a new top-level overlay entry. The root
# .gitignore's secret patterns (*.agekey, keys.txt, *.key, *.dec, *.dec.*, *.plain, *.plain.*, *.bak, *.orig, *~,
# instance/trust/observed-digests.yml, instance/trust/*.gpg) still apply inside these directories: a re-include
# names a directory or a config file, never key material. In the PUBLIC repository no such file exists and the
# whole overlay stays ignored.
!/.gitignore
!/.sops.yaml
!/instance.yml
!/fleet.yml
!/leak-tokens.txt
!/inventory/
!/secrets/
!/dashboards/
!/trust/
# OPERATIONS.md: this deployment's own ledger (boxes, standing rules, owed actions). It holds no secret; it
# never ships, because nothing under instance/ does.
!/OPERATIONS.md
# actuation/<key>/unit.yml + vars.yml: the app-store units the GUI and the API stage. While the directory was
# ignored, `git add` refused the file and no unit could reach a proposal.
!/actuation/
# certs/: PUBLIC material only — a pinned device CA (instance.yml device_trust.<key>.tls_ca_file) and
# known_hosts files, which the deploy reads from this tree. A private key put here by mistake is ignored by its
# name when that is *.key, id_rsa, id_ed25519 or id_ecdsa, and refused by its content at the control node's
# blanket commits when it is PEM or OpenSSH (scripts/lib/plaintext-guard.sh). A binary key store (.p12, .pfx,
# a DER key) or a PuTTY .ppk is caught by neither: do not put one here.
!/certs/
